  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Counterclockwise and Clockwise Rotation",
    "lesson_core_move": "Classify phasor rotation as counterclockwise or clockwise by using the sign of angular frequency to determine the direction of phase progression.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Counterclockwise and Clockwise Rotation",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Counterclockwise and Clockwise Rotation",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When a phasor is written as $e^{j\\theta(t)}$ with $\\theta(t)=\\omega t+\\phi$, read the rotation direction from how the phase angle changes over time. The starting angle comes from $\\phi$; the motion comes from the sign of $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta(t)=\\omega t+\\phi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Take a small positive time step $\\Delta t>0$. Then the phase change is $\\theta(t+\\Delta t)-\\theta(t)=\\omega\\Delta t$, so the sign of the phase change matches the sign of $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta(t+\\Delta t)-\\theta(t)=\\omega\\Delta t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If $\\omega>0$, the angle increases as $t$ increases. Increasing angle means counterclockwise rotation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If $\\omega<0$, the angle decreases as $t$ increases. Decreasing angle means clockwise rotation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the unit phasor $e^{j\\omega t}$ with $\\phi=0$ as the clean base case: it keeps the focus on the sign of $\\omega$ alone. The initial phase only sets the starting angle, and it does not change the direction of rotation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Counterclockwise and Clockwise Rotation",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Positive Angular Frequency as Counterclockwise Rotation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A phasor is given by $e^{j(4t+\\pi/6)}$. Does it rotate clockwise or counterclockwise?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the exponent as the phase angle $\\theta(t)=4t+\\pi/6$. The sign of the coefficient of $t$ is what tells you the direction of rotation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the angular frequency is $\\omega=4$, so it is positive. A positive $\\omega$ means the phase angle increases as $t$ increases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "An increasing phase angle means the phasor moves counterclockwise around the complex plane.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The phase offset $\\pi/6$ only sets the starting angle. It does not change the direction, so $e^{j(4t+\\pi/6)}$ rotates counterclockwise.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Counterclockwise and Clockwise Rotation",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Negative Angular Frequency as Clockwise Rotation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A phasor is given by $e^{j(-5t+\\pi/4)}$. Does it rotate clockwise or counterclockwise?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the exponent as the phase angle $\\theta(t)=-5t+\\pi/4$. The sign of the coefficient of $t$ is what tells you the direction of rotation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the angular frequency is $\\omega=-5$, so it is negative. A negative $\\omega$ means the phase angle decreases as $t$ increases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A decreasing phase angle means the phasor moves clockwise around the complex plane.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The phase offset $\\pi/4$ only sets the starting angle. It does not change the direction, so $e^{j(-5t+\\pi/4)}$ rotates clockwise.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Counterclockwise and Clockwise Rotation",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Classifying Rotation Direction from the Sign of Angular Frequency",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A phasor is written as $e^{-j(3t-\\pi/8)}$. Does it rotate clockwise or counterclockwise after rewriting the exponent?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Rewrite the exponent so the coefficient of $t$ is visible: $e^{-j(3t-\\pi/8)}=e^{j(-3t+\\pi/8)}$. The direction comes from the sign of the $t$-term, not from the phase offset.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "After rewriting, read $\\omega=-3$. Since $\\omega$ is negative, the phase angle decreases as $t$ increases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A decreasing phase angle means the phasor rotates clockwise.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The phase offset $\\pi/8$ only sets the starting angle. It does not change the direction, so the phasor rotates clockwise.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Counterclockwise and Clockwise Rotation",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recognizing That Initial Phase Does Not Change Rotation Direction",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Two phasors are given by $e^{j(4t+\\pi/6)}$ and $e^{j(4t-\\pi/3)}$. Do they rotate in the same direction or different directions?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read both exponents as phase angles: $4t+\\pi/6$ and $4t-\\pi/3$. The coefficient of $t$ is the same in both, so the shared angular frequency is $\\omega=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The phase offsets $\\pi/6$ and $-\\pi/3$ only change the starting angle. They move each phasor to a different initial position, but they do not change how the angle progresses with time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the time-varying part is still $+4t$, the phase angle increases as $t$ increases. An increasing phase angle means counterclockwise rotation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So they rotate in the same direction. Because $\\omega=4>0$ in both cases, both phasors rotate counterclockwise; changing $\\phi$ changes only the starting angle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
